          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice [diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features].\n\nThe new symptom of food sticking, known as dysphagia, is not routinely part of uncomplicated reflux disease [diagnostic, coding-gerd-k21]. The diagnostic specialist states that the \"guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features\" suggests dysphagia is an \"alarm feature\" [diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features]. However, the research specialist notes that while dysphagia generally warrants further investigation, the provided guidance does not explicitly list dysphagia as an \"alarm feature\" in its text [research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features]. Both specialists indicate that dysphagia is a symptom that prompts further clinical consideration [diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features; research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features].\n\nFor adults with typical reflux symptoms but no \"alarm features,\" the usual initial approach is an eight-week trial of acid-reducing medication [diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features; research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features]. The presence of \"alarm features,\" like dysphagia, changes this approach, suggesting that investigation would be considered earlier rather than solely relying on an empiric trial of medication [diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features; research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features].\n\nYou should discuss this new symptom with a clinician [diagnostic, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features; research, guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
